{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-6fcf54bf-73f1-4f57-bdc8-281bc2246457","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a316a8c602900e33","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8006-_ao2k5ka/artifacts/epic-reviews/RHAISTRAT-8006-decomp-review.md","content":"# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8006\n\n## Review Summary\n\nScore: 10/14 — fail\nRecommendation: revise\n\nThe main-plan DAG (E001–E012) is well constructed: HLR coverage is complete,\nimplementation-type parallelism rules are honored (no konflux-onboarding ↔\ngeneral-implementation edges; image digests correctly modeled as AC gates, not\nDAG edges), the docs-authoring priority exception is applied correctly, and the\ngating metadata on E008 is exemplary. The defects are concentrated in the two\nconditional branch epics, which were under-decomposed relative to the main plan:\nthey consume main-plan artifacts (the CSV extension point, the rendered\nmanifests / DSC component configuration surface) without the corresponding DAG\nedges, and they bundle Platform / ODH Operator-owned rhods-operator work into\nKagenti-team epics. Two criteria score 0, so the decomposition fails regardless\nof the total score.\n\n## Criterion Details\n\n### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n\nAll 9 HLRs from the strategy are mapped in the traceability matrix: the five\nP0 HLRs to E004/E006/E008 (with E011 validating cleanup), P1 dashboard/CSV/\nAuthBridge to E010/E009/E007, and the P2 multi-arch HLR is carried as\nexplicitly conditional scope/ACs in E006/E007 (and BRANCH-B-E013) — an\nappropriate disposition for \"arm64 only if free\". Priority inheritance is\ncorrect: E004/E005/E001 (P0) enable P0/P1 work; E012's P0 is the rule-mandated\ndocs-authoring exception (strategy priority Critical → P0) and is correctly\nannotated as not pushing priority upstream. No priority collapse with\ndeferrable features — the P2 multi-arch work bundled into E006/E007 is\nincidental polish on the same build, not a distinct deferrable feature.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n\nThe main-plan DAG is internally consistent: every edge in the summary diagram\nmatches the target epic's frontmatter `dependencies` and vice versa; arrow\ndirection is dependency → dependent; konflux-onboarding epics (E006, E007,\nBRANCH-B-E013) have no edges to or from general-implementation epics; the\nimage-digest coupling to E008/E009 is correctly an AC gate, not an edge;\ncritical path (E004 → E008 → E009 → E011 → E012, length 5) fits the L-size\nheuristic. Branch files both set `branch: B` and `gated_by`, and no main-plan\nepic depends on a branch node.\n\nHowever, scanning epic content for artifact dependencies uncovers two missing\nedges, both critical:\n\n- **BRANCH-B-E013 → E009 (missing).** The branch epic's scope includes \"CSV\n  updates: RELATED_IMAGE entries for the new variants, following the extension\n  point left by the OLM/CSV packaging work\" — an explicit build-against\n  dependency on the CSV artifact E009 produces (the same Rule 13 artifact\n  reasoning the decomposer itself used to justify E008 → E009). Its\n  dependencies list only E002 and E004, so it can be scheduled before the CSV\n  extension point exists.\n- **BRANCH-B-E014 → E008 (missing).** The branch epic's scope includes\n  pulling changes \"into the component's rendered manifests\" and \"any DSC\n  component configuration surface needed to point the trace sink at the\n  platform telemetry infrastructure\" — both are artifacts of E008 (kustomize\n  manifest integration, component CRD/controller). Its dependencies list only\n  E003 and E004.\n\nThe omission is not a convention: the branch DAG does carry a main-plan edge\n(E004 → both branches, justified by the midstream forks), so main-plan →\nbranch edges are clearly permitted and the E008/E009 edges were simply missed.\nBoth are discoverable from epic content, which per the review rules is a\ncritical issue even though diagram and frontmatter agree with each other.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n\n- **Major — wrong team assignment in BRANCH-B-E013.** The epic is team\n  Kagenti / component Agentic, but its CSV-update scope item is rhods-operator\n  work that the strategy's Impacted Teams table assigns to Platform / ODH\n  Operator (\"CSV packaging\") and that this decomposition itself assigns to the\n  Platform team in E009. One epic spanning two components and two teams; the\n  CSV work should be a separate conditional epic (or conditional scope on\n  E009), not bundled into the Kagenti konflux epic.\n- **Major — same pattern in BRANCH-B-E014.** The \"DSC component configuration\n  surface\" scope item is rhods-operator component-controller work owned by\n  Platform / ODH Operator, bundled into a Kagenti-team epic.\n- **Major — E008 is oversized.** The strategy's own effort estimate prices the\n  component-controller work (CRD definition, kustomize manifest integration,\n  garbage-collection logic, DSC schema extension — exactly E008's scope, with\n  E2E testing split off to E011) at 2–3 sprints. That is multiple sprints of\n  work in one epic, exceeding the ~2-week guideline; it is precedented and\n  predictable (16 existing controllers) but should be split (e.g. CRD/schema/\n  registry registration vs. controller pipeline + lifecycle/GC).\n- Minor: E011 and E012 use team names (\"QE\", \"Documentation\") as the\n  `component` field rather than a canonical component.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001 rewrites\nE008's scope (gated_by + fully populated gate_failure_impact with action\n`rewrite`), E002 gates BRANCH-B-E013, E003 gates BRANCH-B-E014. Both branch\nfiles set `branch` and `gated_by` with the gate present in their direct\n`dependencies`, and both carry populated `gate_failure_impact` (action\n`remove` with fallback). Every epic listing an Investigation in `dependencies`\nhas `gated_by` set (E008 → E001; branches → their gates). No Implementation is\nmisclassified; all Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nType-appropriate signal fields are used everywhere: the three Investigations\ncarry `investigation_signals`, all Implementation epics (including branches)\ncarry `ai_signals`; no cross-contamination. Signal values are consistent with\nthe rationales and the strategy content: E001 `source_accessibility: 1`\n(answer readable in public repos) vs. E002/E003 `source_accessibility: 0`\n(pending human decisions, correctly not marked 1); E002/E003\n`human_judgment_required: -2` (pure PM/team decisions); E008\n`open_questions: -1` (gated by E001) while E006/E007 justifiably carry\n`open_questions: 1` (build approach independent of upstream readiness);\nBRANCH-B-E014's uniformly low signals match its undefined shape and\nupstream-first external dependency. Rationales in the ai-signals files match\nthe frontmatter values.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy's proposed acceptance\ncriteria. Rule-mandated ACs are present: E008 carries the disable/rollback\nsafety AC and the Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" AC for the konflux chain;\nE006/E007/BRANCH-B-E013 carry check-payload FIPS and green-build ACs; E012\n(docs-authoring) carries the technical-review AC. The strategy's typo\n`agentrimes.agent.kagenti.dev` is corrected to `agentruntimes...` in E008. No\nepic body references sibling epics by bare draft ID.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nThe epic set collectively accounts for all in-scope strategy scope items\n(Konflux onboarding for both image families, DSC component controller, OLM/CSV\npackaging, dashboard BFF, QE, docs, forks, license validation) and all seven\nproposed acceptance criteria (allocated as documented in the summary).\nStrategy-level context is carried forward: every risk (Konflux lead time,\nupstream gaps, webhook ordering, orphaned resources, port collision) and open\nquestion from the strategy has a documented disposition in the Unknowns\nHandling table and appears in the relevant epic's scope/ACs or signal\nrationales. Documentation Support is required and E012 (docs-authoring)\nexists. Conditional branches cover the bounded outcomes: outcome A\n(proxy-only / OTEL stays with the parent RFE) legitimately requires no\nadditional epics — the main plan already delivers the committed TP minimum —\nand outcome B has an epic file for each gate. Minor: the COULD-level\nacceptance criterion (auto-registration of sandboxed agents) is neither\ncovered nor explicitly deferred, but it is runtime behavior already deferred\nto RHAIRFE-7587 by the strategy's own out-of-scope list.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":55034,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93a87d1d04a08942","agent_id":"aa33879fc1b0e063d","session_id":"7408e02f-2a40-4c49-b134-f5e8ed6e24cb","uuid":"a5bf3149-a0a3-47b4-b66b-f1d3afa49861","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:22:58.297Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8006 decomposition"}
