am'\n36\t- severity: minor\n37\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n38\t  description: E011 and E012 use team names (QE, Documentation) as the component field\n39\t    instead of a canonical component\n40\t- severity: minor\n41\t  criterion: Completeness\n42\t  description: The COULD-level acceptance criterion (auto-registration of sandboxed\n43\t    agents) is neither covered by an epic nor explicitly deferred, though runtime\n44\t    behavior is out of scope per RHAIRFE-7587\n45\terror: null\n46\t---\n47\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8006\n48\t\n49\t## Review Summary\n50\t\n51\tScore: 10/14 — fail\n52\tRecommendation: revise\n53\t\n54\tThe main-plan DAG (E001–E012) is well constructed: HLR coverage is complete,\n55\timplementation-type parallelism rules are honored (no konflux-onboarding ↔\n56\tgeneral-implementation edges; image digests correctly modeled as AC gates, not\n57\tDAG edges), the docs-authoring priority exception is applied correctly, and the\n58\tgating metadata on E008 is exemplary. The defects are concentrated in the two\n59\tconditional branch epics, which were under-decomposed relative to the main plan:\n60\tthey consume main-plan artifacts (the CSV extension point, the rendered\n61\tmanifests / DSC component configuration surface) without the corresponding DAG\n62\tedges, and they bundle Platform / ODH Operator-owned rhods-operator work into\n63\tKagenti-team epics. Two criteria score 0, so the decomposition fails regardless\n64\tof the total score.\n65\t\n66\t## Criterion Details\n67\t\n68\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n69\t\n70\tAll 9 HLRs from the strategy are mapped in the traceability matrix: the five\n71\tP0 HLRs to E004/E006/E008 (with E011 validating cleanup), P1 dashboard/CSV/\n72\tAuthBridge to E010/E009/E007, and the P2 multi-arch HLR is carried as\n73\texplicitly conditional scope/ACs in E006/E007 (and BRANCH-B-E013) — an\n74\tappropriate disposition for \"arm64 only if free\". Priority inheritance is\n75\tcorrect: E004/E005/E001 (P0) enable P0/P1 work; E012's P0 is the rule-mandated\n76\tdocs-authoring exception (strategy priority Critical → P0) and is correctly\n77\tannotated as not pushing priority upstream. No priority collapse with\n78\tdeferrable features — the P2 multi-arch work bundled into E006/E007 is\n79\tincidental polish on the same build, not a distinct deferrable feature.\n80\t\n81\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n82\t\n83\tThe main-plan DAG is internally consistent: every edge in the summary diagram\n84\tmatches the target epic's frontmatter `dependencies` and vice versa; arrow\n85\tdirection is dependency → dependent; konflux-onboarding epics (E006, E007,\n86\tBRANCH-B-E013) have no edges to or from general-implementation epics; the\n87\timage-digest coupling to E008/E009 is correctly an AC gate, not an edge;\n88\tcritical path (E004 → E008 → E009 → E011 → E012, length 5) fits the L-size\n89\theuristic. Branch files both set `branch: B` and `gated_by`, and no main-plan\n90\tepic depends on a branch node.\n91\t\n92\tHowever, scanning epic content for artifact dependencies uncovers two missing\n93\tedges, both critical:\n94\t\n95\t- **BRANCH-B-E013 → E009 (missing).** The branch epic's scope includes \"CSV\n96\t  updates: RELATED_IMAGE entries for the new variants, following the extension\n97\t  point left by the OLM/CSV packaging work\" — an explicit build-against\n98\t  dependency on the CSV artifact E009 produces (the same Rule 13 artifact\n99\t  reasoning the decomposer itself used to justify E008 → E009). Its\n100\t  dependencies list only E002 and E004, so it can be scheduled before the CSV\n101\t  extension point exists.\n102\t- **BRANCH-B-E014 → E008 (missing).** The branch epic's scope includes\n103\t  pulling changes \"into the component's rendered manifests\" and \"any DSC\n104\t  component configuration surface needed to point the trace sink at the\n105\t  platform telemetry infrastructure\" — both are artifacts of E008 (kustomize\n106\t  manifest integration, component CRD/controller). Its dependencies list only\n107\t  E003 and E004.\n108\t\n109\tThe omission is not a convention: the branch DAG does carry a main-plan edge\n110\t(E004 → both branches, justified by the midstream forks), so main-plan →\n111\tbranch edges are clearly permitted and the E008/E009 edges were simply missed.\n112\tBoth are discoverable from epic content, which per the review rules is a\n113\tcritical issue even though diagram and frontmatter agree with each other.\n114\t\n115\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n116\t\n117\t- **Major — wrong team assignment in BRANCH-B-E013.** The epic is team\n118\t  Kagenti / component Agentic, but its CSV-update scope item is rhods-operator\n119\t  work that the strategy's Impacted Teams table assigns to Platform / ODH\n120\t  Operator (\"CSV packaging\") and that this decomposition itself assigns to the\n121\t  Platform team in E009. One epic spanning two components and two teams; the\n122\t  CSV work should be a separate conditional epic (or conditional scope on\n123\t  E009), not bundled into the Kagenti konflux epic.\n124\t- **Major — same pattern in BRANCH-B-E014.** The \"DSC component configuration\n125\t  surface\" scope item is rhods-operator component-controller work owned by\n126\t  Platform / ODH Operator, bundled into a Kagenti-team epic.\n127\t- **Major — E008 is oversized.** The strategy's own effort estimate prices the\n128\t  component-controller work (CRD definition, kustomize manifest integration,\n129\t  garbage-collection logic, DSC schema extension — exactly E008's scope, with\n130\t  E2E testing split off to E011) at 2–3 sprints. That is multiple sprints of\n131\t  work in one epic, exceeding the ~2-week guideline; it is precedented and\n132\t  predictable (16 existing controllers) but should be split (e.g. CRD/schema/\n133\t  registry registration vs. controller pipeline + lifecycle/GC).\n134\t- Minor: E011 and E012 use team names (\"QE\", \"Documentation\") as the\n135\t  `component` field rather than a c