{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-84e14d9b-6968-44e8-b94e-485bd1a8e740","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-978d5691fd5d791d","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8006-_ao2k5ka/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8006-E015.md","content":"# KagentiOperator component controller pipeline and lifecycle\n\n## Description\n\nImplement the KagentiOperator component controller in rhods-operator behind the CRD,\nDSC schema, and registry registration delivered by the component definition work: the\nstandard action pipeline (initialize, releases, kustomize render, deploy, check\ndeployments, garbage collect), the kustomize manifest integration from the midstream\nfork, and the full Managed/Removed lifecycle with garbage collection. This is the\nsecond half of the component controller work, split from the component definition epic\nbecause the strategy prices the combined work at 2–3 sprints — above the ~2-week epic\nguideline.\n\nThis epic is gated on the upstream readiness investigation: if the upstream operator\nhas functional gaps, the controller's manifest set and scope are rewritten (deferred\ncontrollers, different manifest content), or the work escalates to XL with a scope\nreduction.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement the component controller with the standard action pipeline (initialize,\n  releases, kustomize render, deploy, check deployments, garbage collect), wired to\n  the component registry registration.\n- Implement the `managementState` lifecycle: `Managed` deploys the operator with its\n  CRDs, RBAC, and mutating webhook into `redhat-ods-applications` (the operator watches\n  all namespaces for AgentRuntime CRs); `Removed` deletes the controller Deployment,\n  all associated RBAC (ClusterRoles, ClusterRoleBindings, Roles, RoleBindings), the\n  AgentRuntime CRD, the mutating webhook configuration, and namespace-scoped resources\n  in `redhat-ods-applications`.\n- Resolve and document cross-namespace cleanup semantics: what happens to user-created\n  AgentRuntime CRs in other namespaces when the component is disabled (CRD deletion\n  garbage-collects all CRs cluster-wide; the design decision on explicit cross-namespace\n  handling is recorded and tested).\n- Integrate kustomize manifests pulled from the midstream fork at pinned commits into\n  `prefetched-manifests/kagenti-operator/`, with RHOAI-specific overlays for namespace,\n  image references, and RBAC adjustments.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a fresh RHOAI deployment with the kagenti-operator component set to\n  `managementState: Managed`, when the DSC controller reconciles, the kagenti-operator\n  Deployment is created in `redhat-ods-applications` with the Konflux-built image and\n  becomes Ready.\n- Given the component changed from `Managed` to `Removed`, when the controller\n  reconciles, all operator-managed resources (Deployment, RBAC, CRDs including\n  `agentruntimes.agent.kagenti.dev`, webhook configuration) are deleted from the\n  cluster.\n- Given the component is `Managed`, when a user creates an AgentRuntime CR referencing\n  an existing Deployment, the operator applies the `kagenti.io/type: agent` label to\n  the target workload (end-to-end validation of the integrated operator).\n- A Konflux-built image digest for kagenti-operator (and the AuthBridge proxy sidecar)\n  is available and wired into the rendered manifests — configuration reference, filled\n  at execution time.\n- The build pipeline for the onboarded component is green (the Konflux pipeline gate\n  for this integration).\n- Cleanup is tested with resources in multiple namespaces; no orphaned AgentRuntime\n  CRs or webhook configurations persist after disable.\n- Rollback/disable safety: setting the component back to `Removed` fully reverts the\n  cluster to its pre-enable state.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- P0: Operator enabled/disabled via DataScienceCluster CR following the standard\n  component controller pattern — lifecycle enforcement half (RFE acceptance criterion).\n- P0: Full resource cleanup when disabled (RFE acceptance criterion).\n- P0: Tech Preview maturity with explicit opt-in, disabled by default in DSC —\n  enforced by the lifecycle (RFE acceptance criterion).\n- Covers strategy acceptance criteria 1, 2, and 3.\n\n## Risks and Open Questions (in-scope)\n\n- DSC component cleanup leaving orphaned resources — mitigated by an explicit finalizer\n  enumerating owned resources (strategy risk table).\n- kagenti-operator running alongside other platform operators in\n  `redhat-ods-applications` without RBAC conflicts — validated as part of this work\n  (strategy assumption).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":70800,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f9c3a8967f78be5","agent_id":"ae9bc348bba6a12a4","session_id":"7408e02f-2a40-4c49-b134-f5e8ed6e24cb","uuid":"3228cef8-60d8-4be6-accb-505a006f9b46","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:37:03.696Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8006 decomposition"}
