008 → E015 → E009 → E011 → E012 — 6 epics.\n144\t\n145\t## DAG Justification\n146\t\n147\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n148\t| --- | --- | --- |\n149\t| E001 → E008 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates Implementations) | Upstream functional gaps change the component definition's CRD/RBAC surface and scope (which controllers ship, possibly deferring MLflowReconciler / ClientRegistrationController). True gate: `rewrite`. E008 carries `gated_by=E001` and `gate_failure_impact` |\n150\t| E001 → E015 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates Implementations) | Same gate covers the controller pipeline: upstream gaps rewrite the manifest set and which controllers ship. E015 carries `gated_by=E001` and `gate_failure_impact` |\n151\t| E001 ↛ E006/E007 | Rule 17 analog (independent partial value) | Konflux onboarding is procedural with lead time — the strategy mandates immediate submission — and builds whatever source exists in the fork. Not gated by upstream readiness (tiered delivery, see below) |\n152\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 5 (repo-onboarding → konflux-onboarding serial) | The Konflux component builds from the midstream fork; the repo must exist |\n153\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 5 | Same: AuthBridge images build from the kagenti-extensions midstream fork |\n154\t| E004 → E015 | Rule 6 (repo-onboarding → general implementation serial) | The component controller pulls upstream manifests from the midstream fork at pinned commits (edge moved from the pre-split component controller epic to the controller-pipeline half, which owns the manifest integration) |\n155\t| E005 ∥ E004 | Rule 7 (license-validation ∥ repo-onboarding) | Independent inputs — license scanning does not need the fork to exist |\n156\t| E005 ↛ E006/E007 | Rule 8 (license-validation ∥ konflux-onboarding) | Pipeline config does not depend on specific dependency licenses |\n157\t| E005 → E008, E005 → E015 | Rule 9 (license-validation → general implementation serial) | If licenses fail, transitive dependencies change and the deployed manife